module

public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Spectrum.Prime.Topology
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.ZariskisMainTheorem
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Discriminant.TraceElement

/-!
# Existence of finite-open factorizations

A `Stacks.Discriminant.FiniteOpenFactorization A B` is a factorization `A → B' → B` with
`A → B'` finite and `Spec B → Spec B'` an open immersion, recorded in the affine
ring-theoretic form: the induced map of prime spectra is an open embedding, and on every basic
open contained in its image the map of away localizations is bijective.  The relative dualizing
module `ω_{B/A} = Hom_A(B', A) ⊗_{B'} B` of Tag 0BSZ is computed from such a factorization, and
every result about it is stated for a given one.  This file supplies the missing input: a
quasi-finite algebra of finite type *has* a finite-open factorization.

Mathlib's Zariski's main theorem is local at a prime.  At a prime `p` of `B` it produces a
subalgebra `B'ₚ ⊆ B` that is finitely generated as an `A`-module together with an element
`rₚ ∈ B'ₚ ∖ p` for which `B'ₚ[1/rₚ] → B[1/rₚ]` is bijective
(`Algebra.QuasiFiniteAt.exists_fg_and_exists_notMem_and_awayMap_bijective`).  Globalising that
is the content of this file.  The elements `rₚ` generate the unit ideal of `B`, because no
prime of `B` contains all of them, so finitely many of them already do; the `A`-subalgebra `B'`
generated by the corresponding finitely many `B'ₚ` is still finite over `A`, being generated by
finitely many integral elements, and it inherits two properties:

* `B' → B` is injective, and
* for each of the chosen `rᵢ` and every `a : B`, some power of `rᵢ` times `a` lies in `B'`.

Those two, together with `Ideal.span {rᵢ} = ⊤` in `B`, are exactly what the two `Prop`-valued
fields of `FiniteOpenFactorization` need.  The second one says precisely that
`Localization.awayMap` at `rᵢ` is surjective, by `Localization.awayMap_surjective_iff`, so the
hypotheses of the three lemmas below are stated as bijectivity of those away maps.

The mathematics sits in `awayMap_bijective_of_awayCover`.  If `D(g)` lies in the image of
`Spec B` then `g` lies in the radical of `(r₁, …, rₙ)`, hence in the radical of
`(r₁ ^ N, …, rₙ ^ N)` for every `N`; choosing `N` so large that `rᵢ ^ N * a ∈ B'` for all `i`
and writing `g ^ k = ∑ cᵢ rᵢ ^ N` gives `g ^ k * a ∈ B'` as well, which is surjectivity of the
away map at `g`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Discriminant.isOpenEmbedding_comap_of_awayCover`,
  `Stacks.Discriminant.awayMap_bijective_of_awayCover`: the affine open-immersion data for a
  ring map that becomes an isomorphism on a basic-open cover of its source.  Together these are
  the converse of `Stacks.Discriminant.specMap_ulift_isOpenImmersion`.
* `Stacks.Discriminant.exists_finiteOpenFactorization`: Zariski's main theorem in global form,
  i.e. a quasi-finite algebra of finite type admits a finite-open factorization.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 00QC](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00QC)
